# The filtered hashish bar's block faces — the Dry Sifter's resin pass, pressed.
#
# PLACEHOLDER art. Warlon Mhite may redraw it; that is what this file is for.
#
# ## It is hashish_bar's matrix under a blonde palette, and that is deliberate
#
# The two bars are the same block: same 8x4x14 slab, same five cut states, same nine pieces. Drawing
# them as the same surface in two colours is what says so at a glance, and it is also the truth about
# the material — both are pressed sift, and the only difference is how much leaf went through the
# screen with it. Blonde means less oxidation and less plant matter, which is exactly what a second
# pass removes.
#
# COLOUR FOLLOWS PROCESS AND NEVER POTENCY. This one is paler because it is cleaner, not because it
# is stronger; filtered hashish has the same effects at the same amplifiers. A blonde bar that hit
# harder than a brown one would be the same lie as grading three colours weak-to-strong.
#
# Palette is item/filtered_hashish's blonde ramp, flattened for block contrast the same way
# hashish_bar's brown ramp was: vanilla blocks span a median 76 luminance steps against an item's
# 164, so both bars look far less dramatic in the world than the pieces do in a hotbar.
#
# Both faces tile, for the reason hashish_bar's do: the model shrinks along one axis as the bar is
# cut, and its faces take auto-generated UVs, so every cut state samples a different sub-region.

@palette resin
. = none
1 = 6B4E24      the deepest pits in the press
2 = 8A6A33      shadow
3 = A98844      the body of the surface, and the commonest tone
4 = BE9C55      lit body
5 = C9A566      the strain colour itself — the tone the item is keyed to
6 = DCBB7E      pressed sheen, sparse and scattered

@texture block/filtered_hashish_bar_top 16x16 type=block tiling=true
